WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.679
Tools are how an MCP server gives a language model the ability to actually do things.

2
00:00:05.679 --> 00:00:10.640
In this lesson, we'll go through every step of exposing a tool, writing the decorator,

3
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:17.040
specifying the input schema, choosing the right return format, handling errors inside the handler,

4
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.319
and verifying the whole thing works with the MCP inspector before connecting a real client.

5
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.600
You already know what an MCP server is and what the inspector does,

6
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.680
so let's go one level down into the tool itself. A tool definition is what the client actually

7
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:39.680
sees in the capability list. It carries the name, the description the model reads when deciding

8
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:45.919
whether to call, and the input schema describing the arguments. Behind that definition sits the

9
00:00:45.919 --> 00:00:52.880
handler, which is just an ordinary Python function with type annotated parameters. The SDK runs it

10
00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:58.880
when a client invokes the tool. Whatever that function returns becomes the tool result. Return

11
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.879
a plain string when the model needs to read it, or a JSON serializable dictionary when downstream

12
00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:11.279
code needs to parse it. Those pieces are what the rest of this lesson builds. You first create a

13
00:01:11.279 --> 00:01:18.000
server instance by calling the MCP server class with a server name. Then the at mcp.tool decorator

14
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.000
registers a Python function as an MCP tool. Place it directly above the function definition.

15
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.599
The function name becomes the tool name clients see in the capability list. The docstring becomes

16
00:01:29.599 --> 00:01:34.720
the tool description that the model reads to decide whether and when to call it. This makes

17
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:40.559
the docstring one of the most important pieces of text you write for your server. A vague description

18
00:01:40.559 --> 00:01:46.559
like gets data gives the model nothing to work with. A specific description like queries the

19
00:01:46.559 --> 00:01:53.279
ticket system and returns open tickets filtered by assignee and status tells the model exactly

20
00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:58.800
when the tool is appropriate. Invest time in descriptions. They directly affect how reliably

21
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:06.879
the model chooses and uses your tools. Tools return two kinds of output, plain text or structured data.

22
00:02:06.879 --> 00:02:12.399
Return a plain string when the model needs to read the result and compose it into a reply.

23
00:02:13.039 --> 00:02:18.720
Lookup results, generated content, and human readable summaries all fit the text format.

24
00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:25.839
Return a JSON serializable dictionary when the output has multiple fields or when another tool

25
00:02:25.839 --> 00:02:31.520
in the same conversation will consume it. Multi-field records, lists of objects, and

26
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:38.880
paginated results all benefit from structured returns. The SDK serializes dictionaries to JSON

27
00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:44.720
automatically before sending them to the client. Pick the format based on how the result will be

28
00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:51.440
used, not on what's easier to write in the handler. The input schema is what tells the model which

29
00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:58.800
arguments to pass and in what format. The SDK generates a JSON schema automatically from your

30
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:05.199
Python type annotations so you don't write raw JSON yourself. Annotate parameters with standard

31
00:03:05.199 --> 00:03:13.520
Python types, star, int, float, bool, list, and dict all map cleanly. Mark optional parameters with

32
00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:19.039
optional t and provide default values so the model knows it doesn't have to include them.

33
00:03:19.039 --> 00:03:27.199
If you're using Pydantic, add field description to each parameter to include per field documentation

34
00:03:27.199 --> 00:03:32.960
in the schema. The model reads these descriptions when it constructs a tool call, so clear field

35
00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:39.039
descriptions reduce the number of malformed calls you'll see during testing and in production.

36
00:03:40.399 --> 00:03:45.919
Three patterns cover the majority of MCP tools you'll build. The database query pattern accepts

37
00:03:45.919 --> 00:03:52.559
filter parameters, runs a SQL query using an async database driver, and returns rows as a

38
00:03:52.559 --> 00:04:00.320
structured list. The API wrapper pattern accepts request parameters, calls an external HTTP endpoint

39
00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:08.240
using HTTPX or AIO HTTP, and returns the parsed response. The file system access pattern accepts

40
00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:14.800
a path and an operation type, reads or writes using AIO files, and returns the file content

41
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:20.880
or a confirmation string. Each of these follows the same decorator plus handler structure.

42
00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:27.040
What changes is the library you use for the async IO and the schema you define for the inputs.

43
00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:31.519
Recognizing these patterns speeds up tool development considerably.

44
00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:39.119
To signal that a tool failed, you raise an exception rather than return one. For a controlled

45
00:04:39.119 --> 00:04:43.679
client visible message, raise tool error with the text you want the model to see.

46
00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:50.720
Any standard exception works too, and the MCP server class converts it into a tool result marked

47
00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:57.839
with is error set to true. This matters because a returned dict becomes tool content, a successful

48
00:04:57.839 --> 00:05:03.600
result, not an error. So raising is how you actually flag failure, and you don't have to fear

49
00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:09.920
an uncaught exception. The MCP server class automatically catches unhandled exceptions

50
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:15.679
in your handler and returns them as error results, so the server keeps running and other clients

51
00:05:15.679 --> 00:05:22.959
stay connected. Use tool error or mask error details to control exactly what the client sees

52
00:05:22.959 --> 00:05:29.600
when something goes wrong. The MCP inspector is the right tool for testing before you connect a

53
00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:35.760
real client. Run it with npx at model context protocol slash inspector and point it at your

54
00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:41.119
server process. The inspector connects over whatever transport you configured and displays

55
00:05:41.119 --> 00:05:48.799
the full capability list. Every tool, resource, and prompt your server registered. Click any tool to see

56
00:05:48.799 --> 00:05:55.679
its schema, then fill in the input form and submit a test request. The inspector shows you the raw JSON

57
00:05:55.679 --> 00:06:02.559
RPC request and response, so you can verify that the schema matches what you expected, that the

58
00:06:02.559 --> 00:06:08.480
handler runs without errors, and that the return format is what the client will receive. Catching

59
00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:14.079
schema mismatches and handler bugs here is much faster than debugging them through a model

60
00:06:14.079 --> 00:06:20.720
interaction. How you write tool descriptions has a direct effect on how reliably the model uses

61
00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:27.760
your server. Three rules guide good description writing. Be specific. Instead of gets tickets, say

62
00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:34.079
returns a list of open tickets filtered by assignee and status sorted by creation date. State side

63
00:06:34.079 --> 00:06:41.200
effects. If the tool writes data, deletes records, or sends an email, say so explicitly. The model

64
00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:47.200
uses this to avoid triggering mutations when it's only trying to read. List limits, document rate

65
00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:53.279
limits, pagination caps, and size constraints. The model won't guess that a result is capped at 100

66
00:06:53.279 --> 00:06:59.279
rows unless you say so. These three elements, specificity, side effects, and limits are what

67
00:06:59.279 --> 00:07:04.640
separate descriptions that work from descriptions that lead to incorrect or unexpected tool calls.

68
00:07:05.839 --> 00:07:11.279
In production, the failure looks like the model picking the wrong tool or filling in arguments

69
00:07:11.279 --> 00:07:18.079
that don't make sense. That usually traces back to a vague doc string or a loose type annotation,

70
00:07:18.079 --> 00:07:24.559
since those are the only contract the model ever reads. Write the description for someone who can't

71
00:07:24.559 --> 00:07:29.040
see your code, then let the inspector prove it before a client has to.

72
00:07:29.839 --> 00:07:32.559
That's where we'll leave it. I'll see you in the next lesson.

